1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,310
In this simulated town, on average, three quarters of a household's neighbors match its color.

2
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:09,180
How much matching do these households demand?

3
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:12,263
Just a third.

4
00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:16,093
Each household counts the occupied squares around it.

5
00:00:16,393 --> 00:00:22,003
If fewer than a third match, it moves, so it is happy to be outnumbered two to one.

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:27,160
We read real city maps the same way, guessing motives from patterns.

7
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:32,000
So how can a demand of one third build a town of three quarters?

8
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:35,106
Rewind to the start, a random mix.

9
00:00:35,606 --> 00:00:39,536
Here, on average, half the neighbors match, well above a third.

10
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,720
Still, these two hundred thirty-eight are unhappy.

11
00:00:43,166 --> 00:00:50,786
The obvious plan is to move each one to a random empty square where it would be content, so the town stays mixed.

12
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:56,543
Round one takes two hundred moves; the other thirty-eight were content by their turn.

13
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,110
If the plan had worked, this counter would read zero.

14
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:02,220
It reads seventy-four.

15
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,480
Sixty-seven of these were content before round one.

16
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:07,830
They never moved.

17
00:01:08,230 --> 00:01:09,460
Their neighbors did.

18
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,260
Replay one block.

19
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:16,000
At the start, this gold household had three gold neighbors out of eight.

20
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,210
Three eighths beats a third, so it stayed.

21
00:01:19,666 --> 00:01:21,376
Then this neighbor moved away.

22
00:01:21,676 --> 00:01:23,136
Is it still content?

23
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:29,090
Two gold out of seven, less than a third, so now it wants out.

24
00:01:29,533 --> 00:01:31,993
Its neighbor here also has three eighths.

25
00:01:32,293 --> 00:01:36,873
In round two, our household leaves, and this one drops to two sevenths.

26
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:39,603
Now run the whole town.

27
00:01:41,503 --> 00:01:44,193
After round seven, nobody wants to move.

28
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,600
That whole town is one point on this chart.

29
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:54,730
Demand a quarter, and the same starting town barely sorts: fifty-eight percent.

30
00:01:55,230 --> 00:01:58,290
Demand a third, and it jumps to seventy-six.

31
00:01:59,333 --> 00:02:04,033
To see why, sort the random start by each household's share of matching neighbors.

32
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:07,193
With at most eight neighbors, shares come in steps.

33
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:15,073
At a third, everyone left of it is unhappy, including the neighbor that started our block's chain, at two sevenths.

34
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:20,363
Every household at three eighths is one lost match away from two sevenths.

35
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:23,450
At a quarter, how many would start unhappy?

36
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,730
Only these two bars change sides: just ninety-five start unhappy.

37
00:02:30,230 --> 00:02:35,770
Now a household at three eighths can lose a match and stay, so that chain never starts.

38
00:02:37,133 --> 00:02:41,253
In his 1971 paper, Thomas Schelling ran this model.

39
00:02:41,553 --> 00:02:50,763
His households moved to the nearest suitable square, on emptier boards, and there the jump came later, between about a third and a half.

40
00:02:51,866 --> 00:02:55,446
Two simulated towns, both seventy-six percent.

41
00:02:55,946 --> 00:02:58,766
In one town, households demand a third.

42
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:01,726
In the other, nobody minds their neighbors.

43
00:03:02,226 --> 00:03:03,856
Which one wanted a third?

44
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:06,640
This one.

45
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:10,170
The other was placed by a rule, with no preferences.

46
00:03:10,470 --> 00:03:13,050
Real segregation has other causes.

47
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:22,220
One study finds that 1930s federal lending maps increased racial segregation for decades, through reduced credit.

48
00:03:23,033 --> 00:03:24,923
Look again at the first town.

49
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:30,153
As Schelling argued, whatever you guessed its people demanded,

50
00:03:30,453 --> 00:03:32,813
the map alone could not have told you.

